{"type":"message","id":"e737fa4d","parentId":"3c1d02f2","timestamp":"2026-10-28T22:02:23.818Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting: data includes all terms across 2015-2016, so 2016 terms appear in 2015 too (some like boolin, woke, broccoli already nonzero).\n\nHmm, maybe the story's ranking lists were based on a national Google Trends pull with years 2013-2016, and the CSV provides only these terms' state values, not the national. Thus ranking may be reproducible as national average? But our 2016 ranking doesn't match.\n\nWait — maybe the national search interest is the SUM across states (total searches), not mean. Let's compute ranking by sum (which weights big states). Sum each week across states, but missing values cause differences. Better: use state-level 2016 total searches (sum over all weeks) — this weights states by population implicitly? No, sum over states of per-capita index isn't population weighted, but bigger states contribute more rows potentially.\n\nActually missing data: small states may have missing values more often (low volume). Let's check which states have NaNs and how many.\n\nBut \"search interest per capita\" as a Google Trends index: each state-week value is per capita relative to overall max. National per-capita = population-weighted average of state per-capita values. Sum over states of per-capita values weights big states more (population by count). The mean weights equally. Sum ranking would approximate population weighting better? Sum of per-capita across states = population-weighted total if each state's population ~ equal? No: national per capita = Σ(pop_i * rate_i)/Σpop_i. Sum of rate_i is wrong unless each state's population equal. But with 51 states, sum gives weight to more populous states in the sense of more rows? No, each state one row per week. Sum treats all states equally too.\n\nThe right weighting needs population, which isn't in the data. So national ranking from this file cannot exactly match Google's national pull.\n\nMaybe the intended reproducible findings are the geographic ones (state comparisons) and the ratio.\n\nLet's inspect the state-level stories.\n\nWoke geography: \"early signs of 'woke' in liberal-leaning blue states, such as Vermont, Massachusetts, Rhode Island, New York, and California. Relative to other states, folks in the south (specifically South Carolina and Arkansas) are falling behind.\"\n\nHow to test: 2016 average per state of woke, or growth slope per state. \"Early signs\" might mean the level in early 2016. Let's compute mean 2016 by state and rank; see whether VT, MA, RI, NY, CA are top and SC, AR at bottom.\n\nSJW: Oregon and Vermont growing. Compute slope per state or 2016 mean.\n\nBoolin: south. Compute mean by state and see top states (southern).\n\nShook: California this summer. Compute mean in summer 2016 by state; CA highest?\n\nBough: Mississippi. Compute 2016 mean by state; MS highest?\n\nLet's check.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_q9xIOaqQfWjJKx53HJKn9159","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/user/RL_Study/report-gepa/runs/originals/new-slang && python - <<'EOF'\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nd=pd.read_csv('data/state-weekly-searches.csv')\nd['dt']=pd.to_datetime(d.date,format='%m/%d/%y')\nnum=[c for c in d.columns[2:] if c!='dt']\nd16=d[d.dt.dt.year==2016]\nfor t in ['woke','SJW','boolin','shook','bough']:\n    m=d16.groupby('region')[t].mean().sort_values(ascending=False)\n    print(t,'2016 mean top10'); print(m.head(10).round(3).to_string())\n    print('bottom10'); print(m.tail(10).round(3).to_string())\n    print()\nEOF"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-flash","usage":{"input":1761,"output":842,"cacheRead":26752,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":29355,"cost":{"input":0.00024654,"output":0.00023576,"cacheRead":0.00007490559999999999,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0005572056000000001}},"stopReason":"toolUse"