{"type":"message","id":"6ea0c7a7","parentId":"b98daf77","timestamp":"2026-10-28T22:02:32.201Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting. Shook slope: CA #1 — matches \"blew up in California this summer\". Bough: MS #1 — matches \"thing in Mississippi\". SJW slope: OR #2 but VT? not top 10. Hmm story says \"growing in left-leaning states such as Oregon and Vermont\" — OR yes; VT not top 10 by slope. Maybe by change from beginning 2016 to end 2016? Let's compute. Or maybe the map colors by \"percent growth\" (ratio end/start), where VT might rank high. Let's compute.\n\nBoolin: \"growth firmly rooted in the south\" — by slope, SD #1, NH, WI; southern states not dominant. Hmm by level, IL, WY, DE, SD; not south. Maybe the map is of a specific measure (e.g., growth rate early period). Let's examine.\n\nWait — maybe I should find the actual charts. The story's embedded visualizations may have state maps with color scales and legends stating values. web_view didn't show them. Maybe the page is archived elsewhere (e.g., web.archive.org) and scripts might render. Let's try fetching with web_view the wayback version? Or look at the HTML via a proxy like r.jina.ai.\n\nTry web_fetch \"https://r.jina.ai/https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/\".","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_0VIHC4vZP2cViLUMLiPw0578","name":"web_fetch","arguments":{"url":"https://r.jina.ai/https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01_YjRBnlyMk6T0PUow2CnN1677","name":"web_fetch","arguments":{"url":"http://archive.org/wayback/available?url=pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-flash","usage":{"input":898,"output":409,"cacheRead":30464,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31771,"cost":{"input":0.00012572,"output":0.00011452000000000001,"cacheRead":0.0000852992,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00032553920000000003}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1793224948844,"responseId":"96ea1856-632b-413d-bb91-638994fd44f0"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b92d9882","parentId":"6ea0c7a7","timestamp":"2026-10-28T22:02:40.361Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_00_0VIHC4vZP2cViLUMLiPw0578","toolName":"web_fetch","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Title: Where New Slang Comes From\nURL Source: https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/\nMarkdown Content:\nIn 2014, I [researched](http://poly-graph.co/shorty.html) the musical origin of the word “shorty.” Turns out that Too $hort was the first to rap it and Lil Jon coined the southern-drawled “shawty.”\nShorty wasn’t pervasive, however, until 50 Cent intro’d [In Da Club](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5qm8PH4xAss) with _“Go shorty. It’s your birthday.”_\n50 Cent, arguably, made “shorty” mainstream slang.\nIdentifying these moments is fascinating: the **tipping point** for when slang reaches the masses. If 2015 was the year of “Netflix and chill” and 2014 was “on fleek,” what slang tipped in 2016?\nUsing Google searches for words’ definitions, I examined **slang that was once rarely searched but is now relatively popular.** Here’s what blew up last year:\nBy “searches for a word’s definition” I mean a search for [word] + “define”, “dictionary”, or “definition.” This list excludes proper nouns and acronyms ([full list here](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1reOFVs8RRCq06ZKXpXegFxlRw45B7wapJfrmz22q5gQ/edit) if you’d like to battle linguistics). To remove words attached to the news cycle, I filtered for terms that consistently rose in popularity throughout the year (which excludes terms such as “electoral college,” which spiked suddenly in November).\nIf you’re over 25 years old, these words still might seem foreign. While they’ve “tipped,” they’re still in an early stage of their lifecycle. Since I can decipher most of the slang from 2013 - 2015, perhaps 2016’s words need a few more months to bake before reaching elder Millennials and Gen X’rs.\nWhy did these words have an “In Da Club” moment and surge in popularity? I did some digging on 2016’s slang; here are three obvious catalysts:\nThree of 2016’s top words are rooted in hip hop. There’s a long history of hip hop and slang: pre-Internet, it was arguably one of the richest transcriptions of [African American Vernacular English](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_American_Vernacular_English) (the source for copious English slang). Let’s examine the songs responsible for each word.\nBroccoli (noun) - weed\nProps to rapper [D.R.A.M.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D.R.A.M.): he single-handedly transformed “broccoli” into popular slang (#4 on 2016’s list). Ever since D.R.A.M. released the the song [“Broccoli”](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K44j-sb1SRY) in April 2016, searches for the word’s definition have climbed to about 75% of where “Netflix and chill” peaked in 2015.\nCheck out the growth for \"Broccoli\" in the red line below.\nIt’s worth noting that D.R.A.M. did not coin “broccoli” as slang; E-40 [used it](http://www.xxlmag.com/video/2016/12/e-40-speaks-up-broccoli-slang/) at least 10 years prior (as did [Raekwon in ‘97](https://genius.com/670003)). But as with 50 Cent and “shorty,” D.R.A.M. pushed the word into mainstream English.\nJuju (noun) - a dance\n“Juju On That Beat” by Zay Hilfigerrr has over 100 million streams [on Spotify](https://open.spotify.com/track/1lItf5ZXJc1by9SbPeljFd). “Juju” [refers](https://genius.com/Zay-hilfigerrr-juju-on-that-beat-tz-anthem-lyrics) to a dance, so linguists could argue that it’s a proper noun and not slang (but then what is “twerk”?). That said, it’s #3 on 2016’s list, a testament to hip hop’s effect on language.\nWavy (adj.) - progressive, cool, swag, well-dressed\n“Wavy” has been around for many years, but only recently surged in popularity. All signals suggest that [Ty Dolla $ign’s track “Wavy”](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Uc-apOIYbFM) was the cause, which dropped in early 2016, coinciding with the subsequent growth of the word (though it had been locally popular in Virginia well beforehand).\nTy Dolla $ign’s “Wavy” was never that popular (it didn’t even chart), and searches for wavy’s definition continue to grow. So unlike “In Da Club,” I think of Ty’s track as an initial spark rather than _the cause_. For all the kids using “wavy,” few probably know its roots (Max B. coined it) or that Ty Dolla $ign had any role in its recent popularity.\nIn the US, last year’s political circus tipped five of 2016’s top slang into the mainstream.\nWoke (noun) - aware of (racial) social injustice\n“Woke” is the one term on 2016’s list that’s been growing organically for at least a decade. There was no major event that propelled it into the spotlight – it just finally crossed the threshold of mainstream use.\n![Image 1](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/woke.png)\nThere were some unique events in 2016 for “woke.” In terms of semantics, there’s the notion that 2016’s US election cycle whitewashed the term. Today, I see it used broadly as “political awareness,” beyond its historic racial connotations. [Childish Gambino’s “Redbone”](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kp7eSUU9oy8) helped too (~50M streams on Spotify), which dropped in November 2016. The song’s hook repeats the phrase “stay woke,” helping to normalize the phrase.\nGeographically, you’ll find early signs of “woke” in liberal-leaning blue states, such as Vermont, Massachusetts, Rhode Island, New York, and California. Relative to other states, folks in the south (specifically South Carolina and Arkansas) are falling behind on the “woke” trend.\n![Image 2](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/woke_geo.jpg)\nTriggered (verb) - “getting filled with hate after seeing, hearing or experiencing something you can't stand” (via [Urban Dictionary](http://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=Triggered))\nThe word “triggered” has existed for years in psychology, short for [“trauma triggers”](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_trigger) (e.g., gunshots for a veteran who suffers from PTSD). Recently, this evolved into the politically divisive “trigger warnings” for books, films, and discussions that may trigger similar distress.\n“Triggered” has now evolved into a quasi liberal-directed insult, effectively meaning “emotional meltdown.” For example, [this Breitbart headline](http://www.breitbart.com/tech/2016/11/09/triggered-students-across-country-melt-down-in-response-to-trump-victory/) following Trump’s election victory, “**Triggered** Students Across Country Melt Down in Response to Trump Victory.”\nGaslighting (verb) - distorting/disputing facts and events\nThe etymology of “gaslighting” begins with the 1938 play “Gas Light.” For several decades, it typically referred to a form [domestic emotional abuse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaslighting) where the victim is given false information so that they question their own memory and reality.\nThe word’s recent prominence is due to Trump’s use of facts (i.e., alternative facts, fake news, post-truth) or conflicting versions of events (e.g., the inauguration crowd size). “Gaslighting” is now a [liberal critique](http://www.teenvogue.com/story/donald-trump-is-gaslighting-america) of Trump’s disagreement with the media over what happened versus what didn’t.\nBigly (adj.) - big time\nDuring the US presidential debates, Donald Trump’s use of “big league” was heard as “bigly,” thus creating a meme and a new word for both anti- and pro-Trump supporters.\nSJW (noun) - Social Justice Warrior\nSJW is an acronym for social justice warrior. It is now a derogatory term, often wielded against liberals. Here’s a [great Urban Dictionary entry](http://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=SJW), “someone who repeatedly and vehemently engages in arguments on social justice on the Internet, often in a shallow or not well-thought-out way, for the purpose of raising their own personal reputation.” Similar to “woke,” it’s been slowly growing organically, and the 2016 election cycle likely tipped it over the threshold for mainstream use.\nAmong political words, it’s also one of the youngest terms on 2016’s list.\n![Image 3](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/sjw.png)\nAnd because of its liberal nature, we can see it growing in left-leaning states such as Oregon and Vermont.\n![Image 4](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/sjw_geo.jpg)\nDank meme (noun) - an (ironically) high-quality meme\nThe etymology of “dank meme” is complex, though Reddit is where it initially found momentum. What’s important is that it spread beyond the underbelly of Reddit sometime last year. While “dank” was initially used ironically, it’s now taken literally as “high-quality” by a more basic audience.\nHolosexual (noun) - strong attraction to holographic objects\n“Holo” and “Holosexual” took off last year, largely due to YouTuber [Simply Nailogical](https://www.youtube.com/user/simplynailogical)’s [obsession](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxUnwCziDso) with holographic nail art.\nSadly, I’m unable to find the causes for the popularity of three words.\nBoolin (verb) - chilling\nThe origin of “boolin” is the [Bloods](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloods)’ habit of replacing c’s with b’s (cool becomes bool). It is at least four years old – I found use of “boolin” by rapper YG [in 2012](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPsrqXSyceg). What’s interesting is that the word’s recent growth is firmly rooted in the south.\n![Image 5](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/boolin_geo.jpg)\nThe problem is that I can’t figure out why. Atlanta-based rapper Young Thug [uses](https://twitter.com/youngthug/status/205042307911794688?lang=en)[“boolin”](https://genius.com/10290480)[quite](https://genius.com/Young-thug-best-friend-lyrics)[often](https://genius.com/Young-thug-power-lyrics) in his music, but I can’t reason that this is the cause for its growth (and subsequent national spread).\n[Update: I was just informed that last year had two Instagram memes with boolin: [“boolin in the butt”](https://twitter.com/SexualGif/status/779161884025028609) and [“boolin on mars.”](https://onsizzle.com/i/my-blood-homie-said-he-was-boolin-in-the-butt-1001758) Both memes are pretty obscure (even when searching Instagram), but could have played a role in boolin’s rise.]\nShook (adj.) - scared\nShook has been around [for decades](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PosV5zVNWlc). Yet for some reason, the word blew up in California this summer.\n![Image 6](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/shook_geo.jpg)\nBough (?)\nI have no idea what this means and why it grew in popularity. It’s a thing in Mississippi though.\n![Image 7](https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/bough_geo.jpg)\nIf you have any leads on boolin, shook, or bough, hit me up: matt@pudding.cool or tweet at [@puddingviz](https://twitter.com/puddingviz)\n\n## Links\n"}],"details":{},"isError":false,"timestamp":1793224960361}}
